# Kernel configuration for the busybox-init flavor.
#
# busybox-init's whole boot path is: kernel mounts devtmpfs/proc/sysfs (all
# in base.config already) -> busybox init (PID 1) reads /etc/inittab ->
# runs /etc/init.d/rcS (mounts tmpfs on /run and /tmp, sets the hostname) ->
# spawns getty/login/ash on the serial console (see package/busybox-init.sh).
# None of that needs cgroups, namespaces, POSIX message queues, BPF,
# debugfs/tracefs, configfs or AF_UNIX/networking — those are systemd's own
# IPC/sandboxing plumbing (see systemd.config) and busybox's init/getty/
# login/ash applets never touch them. So there is nothing to turn on here:
# base.config + <platform>.config + <arch>.config already provide every
# option this flavor's boot path exercises, in particular:
#   - CONFIG_TMPFS=y            (base.config) - rcS's "mount -t tmpfs ... /run /tmp"
#   - CONFIG_DEVTMPFS[_MOUNT]=y (base.config) - /dev populated with no mdev/udev running
#   - CONFIG_PROC_FS=y          (base.config) - rcS's "mount -t proc ... /proc"
#   - CONFIG_SYSFS=y            (base.config) - rcS's "mount -t sysfs ... /sys"
#   - CONFIG_TTY / serial console (base.config + <platform>.config) - getty/login
#
# This file exists (rather than skipping the fourth fragment for this
# flavor) so init-flavor selection in linux.sh's prepare() stays plain
# name-keyed path assembly, and so a future busybox-side kernel need has an
# obvious, self-contained home — exactly like network.config would if a
# later change wants opt-in networking independent of init flavor.
